  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 307 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Early morning surface analysis had a weak nearly \nstationary cold front from northern Bolivar county to southern \nLowndes county. Local radars showed the last of a few showers \nassociated with the cold front shifting into western Alabama. The \nweak cold front will remain the focus for additional convection \nthrough tonight but, primarily this afternoon during peak heating. \nThe cold front is expected to drift farther south through our CWA \nand become nearly diffuse tonight. A surface high was noted centered \nto our north and to our south with the frontal boundary in between. \nEarly morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis still showed our CWA \nlocked between a large mid level ridge to our west and a trough to \nour east. These features won't change much through tonight. So, the \nfocus for today will again be the heat and humidity. Thanks to the \npresence of the cold front our northeast will see a little reprieve \nfrom the oppressive heat but the remainder of the CWA peak heat \nindex values will be between 105F and 110F again. A Heat Advisory \nremains in effect from 10am-7pm for this area. /22/\n\nFriday Onwards...\n\nThe long-term forecast remains on track: Increased chances for \nconvective activity and cloud coverage will take hold due to the \nsurface confluence from the aforementioned front, which could \nprovide a few degree dip, thus mitigating heat threat concerns. With \nupper flow becoming more northerly and northwesterly during this \ntime frame, we will see the potential for one or two convective \ncomplexes moving into the area or developing over the area. This \nwill result in higher diurnal rain chances across the area Friday \nheading into the weekend. Going into early next week, and conditions \nbecome less favorable we'll see rain chances diminish and heat \npotentially rebuild some, but remain below what we've seen over the \nlast few days./OAJ/",
